{
  "description": "Which raw arguments a read may be cached under (#271), written by hand. Go keys both read caches from what its reader makes of the arguments and runs the tool with the bytes the model wrote, so arguments that reader does not say exactly would share a key while running differently: [1] and [2] both read as {}, 1e400 and 2e400 both as null, 9007199254740993 and 9007199254740992 as one float64. Such a read gets no key and is kept in neither cache. In cases, text is one call's argument text exactly; go is what ReadCacheKeyOf gives for those bytes, typescript what readCacheKey gives for what JSON.parse reads from them (a text JSON.parse refuses never reaches it, and reads as no key). Each outcome is the canonical JSON of the key and its SHA-256, or key none. The two differ only where JavaScript has already read the number or the name and runs the tool with what it read, so what it keys is what runs. items are the key objects the ordinary rows make. context/readKeys.test.ts and go/context/read_arguments_test.go hold the readers to every case, and tests/test_contract.py derives each outcome from its text.",
